      body: "Second of the five Bennet daughters, twenty years old at the start of the novel, the most quick-witted and morally serious member of her family. She is not the most beautiful — that is her older sister Jane — and she is not the most accomplished, and she has no fortune. What she has is a good eye, a fast intelligence, a sense of humour that occasionally tips into satire at the expense of people who deserve it, and a vanity about her own perceptiveness that the novel gradually exposes and corrects. Elizabeth is one of the most fully realised heroines in nineteenth-century fiction, and the long tradition of writing about her — from Charlotte Brontë's complaints to Virginia Woolf's praise to the modern adaptations — is part of why the novel has remained continuously read.",
